#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b6b8a0-8e4b-51b5-b992-659f82730241" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: First or Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "d5dc3dd6-6366-5cb9-9deb-7f4c71de0bba" :question/math-academy-id "e-5077" :question/problem "Express the Cartesian point $P (1,-\\sqrt{3})$ in polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "The first thing to realize is that the point $P$ lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/935/Source/Images/353b325d00952160420a21721fdb8c2c.png)\n\nFirst, we determine $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} r &= \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{1^2 + (-\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\[3pt] &=\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\[3pt] &= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute the reference angle $\\theta_R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\theta_R &= \\left\\begin{vmatrix}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right)\\right & \\\\ &= \\left & \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1}\\right)\\right & \\\\ &= \\left & \\arctan\\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right)\\right & \\\\ &= \\left & -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point lies in the fourth quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(2, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "51309671-3a82-582f-93b0-e30c49dcaf7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5078" :question/problem "Express the Cartesian point $P (0,-7)$ in polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} r &= \\sqrt{0^2 + (-7)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{7^2} \\\\ &= 7 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of the $y$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(7, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091978 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "54f6db85-a16b-5e8c-bd8d-caccc6f3563e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5079" :question/problem "Express the Cartesian point $P (-6, 8)$ in polar coordinates. Round your answer to three decimal places where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/935/Source/Images/40b1c3db885180812cfbf4edd958ae6a.png)\n\nFirst, we determine $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} r &= \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + 8^2} \\\\[3pt] &=\\sqrt{100} \\\\[3pt] &= 10 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\theta_R &= \\left\\begin{vmatrix}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right)\\right & \\\\[3pt] &= \\left & \\arctan\\left(-\\dfrac 8{6}\\right)\\right\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[3pt] &\\approx 0.927\\,29. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π - 0.92729 \\\\\n&\\approx 2.21429 \\\\\n&\\approx 2.214\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places. Therefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(10,2.214)$."}]}
